RK628F backport for Aico Link SCH v1
==============================

Source: https://github.com/rockchip-linux/kernel
Branch: develop-5.10
Commit: 81417fbf13edb25f203ea794a723b8bf1f457d5c
Directory: drivers/media/i2c/rk628
Dependency: include/uapi/linux/rk_hdmirx_config.h

The original BSP driver only implements RK628D. Aico Link silicon reports
0x20230321 at GRF_SOC_VERSION (0x0200), identifying RK628F. Backport the
coherent receiver, CRU, MIPI PHY and post-processing implementation together.

Local integration differences:
- Restore the probe-time 1194 MHz CPLL reference on resume after power loss,
  before initializing HDMI timing measurement and output clocks.
- Expose optional attributes on the I2C device, without a newer global
  Rockchip HDMI class dependency.
- Omit the external RKCIF soft-reset hook unavailable in this BSP. Bridge
  CSI recovery remains enabled. RKCIF is a separate loadable module.
- Default to Aico Link's two-block digital HDMI 1080p60 EDID. Userspace can
  persistently select 1080p30 for sources that lose data-channel lock at
  148.5 MHz; this retains full resolution at a 74.25 MHz pixel clock.
- Serialize EDID reads with receiver recovery and writes. Return EAGAIN
  during configuration and propagate I2C errors instead of returning bad data.
- Serialize userspace EDID updates and restart receiver training afterwards.
- Return ENOLCK for absent video instead of reporting fallback VGA timings.
- Guard optional IRQs, retain V4L2 client data, lock control updates and
  observe live AVI readiness during polling.
- Retry failed receiver training every 10 seconds without a plugin GPIO.
  SCH v1 routes Plugin_DET to the MCU; no RV1106 GPIO is invented here.
- Debounce F receiver timing interrupts against the last configured input,
  including link lock and color metadata, before stopping a working stream.
  Ignore unchanged-mode measurement jitter with 0.1% pixel-clock tolerance.
  Retry transient channel-lock drops over 80 ms before declaring signal loss.
- Mask the parent HDMI interrupt while changing leaf masks or recovering;
  publish readiness before unmasking it, preserving independent CSI routing.
  Serialize mask updates with the threaded HDMI handler using an IRQ mutex.
- Return EAGAIN from timing queries while receiver configuration is busy,
  instead of blocking userspace on the recovery mutex.
- Require live clock and data-channel lock around every F receiver timing
  sample. AVI mode lookup can otherwise report stable 1080p60 after pixel
  transport has lost lock. Clear failed timing results and preserve I2C errors.
- Log timing-query MIPI mode selection only at debug level. Routine readiness
  queries repeat this selection even when the input and transport are unchanged.
- Serialize stream transitions and format readiness with receiver recovery.
  Reject unavailable or interrupted stream starts and disable partial CSI
  setup before returning an error to the capture pipeline.
- Propagate CSI PHY lock and lane stop-state polling errors through STREAMON.
  Keep streaming/power state clear on failure and disable partial setup before
  returning the original error so capture can retry.
- Sample CED once per receiver configuration so updating its error baseline
  cannot suppress the retry required after an equalizer adjustment.
- Do not sample CED at stream start. CED reads can change EQ based on
  historical idle errors and disrupt a locked input. Keep EQ changes in
  receiver training rather than stream transitions.

The DTS retains the onboard 24 MHz oscillator, GPIO1_A0 power enable,
active-low GPIO1_A1 reset, GPIO1_B1 IRQ and four continuous-clock CSI lanes.
No GPIO override is used for HDMI HPD.
